{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices","id":1117379,"title":"Ditching Traditions/Crafts for just Practices?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-17T12:50:00-07:00","id":1117379,"title":"Ditching Traditions/Crafts for just Practices?","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:verdana\"><span style=\"color:#222222\">What would you guys think about getting rid of the specific Traditions/Crafts and just having the Traditions (faction) being a hodgepodge of mystic &amp; technomagic practices? There would still be a Council of Nine, with each holding one of the sphere associated seats, but they wouldn't be automatically the big powerhouse groups. I like the idea of the Traditions (faction) be more inclusive. It also seems to me that the Traditions/Crafts fill a similar role as Practice. And since Practices are more diverse, the obvious solution is to get rid of the Traditions/Crafts and let Practices be the divisions of what kind of magic you do. Obviously this might not be for everyone, but it feels right for my vision of Mage.<br /> <br /> <b>NEVER MIND</b></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:13px\">For anyone who doesn't see my later post, I've been convinced that my viewpoint was flawed and I've since decided to keep the Traditions around.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1885,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","user":"CrackedMan","date":". 07-21-2017, 11:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","name":"CrackedMan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1885_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"254"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T13:30:00-07:00","id":1117406,"title":"","content":"<br /> The Order of Hermes already brings together a lot of practices, so you could try their model. There are many houses and a few core beliefs, but Criamon from Ex Miscellena, Flambeau, Xaos (formerly Thig) and House Shaea are quite different but are all Order of Hermes. Other Traditions, Crafts, Technocracy, and Nephandi are groupings of like-minded people with similar practices and histories. <br /> <br /> If you have a Council of Nine, with a seat tied to a group, then if the Council of Nine is relevant, who sits on it is relevant. If the Council of Nine is mere window dressing, it doesn't really matter what the Traditions are. However, eliminating the history and politics of the Traditions would create a different tone to the game. Moving from Mage 1e to Mage Revised was watching the change in Tradition and Technocracy politics as events happened. Doisstep is very different without all the Tradition infighting and skullduggery.<br /> <br /> However, not all games are tied to large-scale politics of the mage factions, so in some games a change in how the Traditions work isn't really that impactful.<br /> <br /> It's your game, so what's correct is what works for your table.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T13:45:17-07:00","id":1117414,"title":"","content":"Not really my thing. But it could work if you wanted to.<br /> <br /> The biggest problem is that getting rid of the groups means getting rid of cultures for characters to engage with and be part of.<br /> <br /> Without cultural context, there's no reason why different mages with the same Practice would get along. Dedicated pantheists in the Verbena and Euthanatos, animist Dreamspeakers, and monotheist Choristers might all have the Faith Practice. Take out the Traditions and... why would any of those form up as a social unit that then teaches new mages a style of magic?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T14:01:41-07:00","id":1117427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1117406#post1117406\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, not all games are tied to large-scale politics of the mage factions, so in some games a change in how the Traditions work isn't really that impactful.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, basically this is it. The political backstabbing stuff has never interested me. I get enough of that in VtM.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1117414#post1117414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not really my thing. But it could work if you wanted to.<br /> <br /> The biggest problem is that getting rid of the groups means getting rid of cultures for characters to engage with and be part of.<br /> <br /> Without cultural context, there's no reason why different mages with the same Practice would get along. Dedicated pantheists in the Verbena and Euthanatos, animist Dreamspeakers, and monotheist Choristers might all have the Faith Practice. Take out the Traditions and... why would any of those form up as a social unit that then teaches new mages a style of magic?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bThe culture isn't dependent on the Traditions. Those are just formalized splat groups because WW liked having splats. You can still have groups who practice witchcraft without the Verbena. You can still have social units who teach each other without the term Tradition. Why would they NOT group together and associate with people who do similar types of magic? That'd be like saying that people wouldn't gather to play football together if the NFL didn't exist. They would, just not on a large formal scale.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","name":"CrackedMan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1885_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"254"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-17T20:01:31-07:00","id":1117561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CrackedMan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1117427#post1117427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bThe culture isn't dependent on the Traditions.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, but Traditions/etc. are the source of mage-cultures in the game as is. If you remove them and don't replace them, nothing fills that role any more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can still have groups who practice witchcraft without the Verbena. You can still have social units who teach each other without the term Tradition. Why would they NOT group together and associate with people who do similar types of magic? That'd be like saying that people wouldn't gather to play football together if the NFL didn't exist. They would, just not on a large formal scale. </div> </div> </div>Yes, you don't need the Verbena to have witches, because not all witches are Verbena and not all Verbena are witches. The problem is that mages of a shared Sleeper cultural background are going to congregate over that more than Practices. A Druidic mage practicing Witchcraft, a Druidic mage practicing Faith, and a Druidic mage praticing High Ritual, are going to gather together for their shared Druidism. The Druidic Faith mage isn't going to go hang out with an Evangelical Faith mage because of Faith.<br /> <br /> Or in different terms, Paradigm and Instruments matter too. Once you have a social unit that creates mage-cultures via a common set of closely related Paradigms, a few compatible Practices, and shared style of Instruments... you have the Traditions and Crafts even if you don't call them that. Then removing the names of the groups is just a cosmetic change, not a meaningful one.<br /> <br /> And that's the thing. &quot;Practice&quot; doesn't imply anything close to a similar type of magic, because Focus as a whole describes a type of magic and even then ten characters that take exactly the same Focus can still have meaningful differences in type of magic. The NFL thing is off. What you're doing, is saying that if you take away all the names of individual sports and call them all Sport, all athletes will hang out because of their shared participation in Sport; after all swimmers, wrestlers, and baseball players are the same Sport player types.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T07:09:14-07:00","id":1117710,"title":"","content":"Yup.<br /> <br /> A Christian Gnostic that does do High Ritual and a High Ritual Brahamin are going to have more in common with Faith, or even Maleficia, people of their cultures than with each other.<br /> <br /> A Faith mage that believes in Returning to the Golden Age isn't going to have the same interests as the one who has Faith in that the World it's an Illusion. It has sense for people of very different Practices to join together into &quot;Bringing Back the Golden Age&quot; as long as they kinda agree in what that Age it's, and if you put syncretism into the plate it has every sense for witches, mystics, wizards and even scientists of very different cultural Background to put their differences appart.<br /> <br /> In the end, Traditions aren't just amalgamation of Practices, they also share a history, objectives, and a few Paradigms (I.E: Euthanatos have several distinct Practices and Paradigms, but all care about justice, retribution, death)... If you loose this common threads, it happens something similar as with Mage: The Awakening, where mages mystical paths are very personal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-18-2017, 10:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T08:46:08-07:00","id":1117770,"title":"","content":"I think it's absolutely doable, but it does make for a pretty substantial change in the setting. There were a lot of reasons, internal and external, for why the Traditions formed the way they did. (Not in the least, the growing threat of the Order of Reason.)<br /> <br /> It might be worth looking at Sorcerer's Crusade, which gives a more in depth look at the mage setting at or around the founding of the Traditions (in their more modern incarnation, because obviously there are parts of each Tradition that are much more ancient). Even if you don't intend to use the Traditions as presented, the information might be useful in helping establish details for your own set-up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T09:20:49-07:00","id":1117798,"title":"","content":"In my game, I made the decision to replace the Traditions, as I feel they didn't age particularly well. I got more work than I bargained for, really. I didn't fully consider how many decisions I would have to make, and how much writing I would need to do for my players' benefit. I still like where it's going, and it's really pushing me as a ST, but it's work.<br /> <br /> So, while I sympathize with the desire to remove the Traditions, I can't imagine running a game without <b>something </b>to replace them. Practices are a great addition to the game, but they don't answer key questions:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What does the character believe is the appropriate use of magick?</li> <li>What change does the character want to see in the world? Mages by and large are not content with the status quo, and in Mage ambition influences magick.</li> <li>What is the underlying belief structure that fuels their study of magick? Being a Mage is dangerous and difficult; most aren't in it just for personal power. Compare the reasoning of a Verbena to a Euthanatos.</li> <li>What is the method of training apprentices, and do experienced Mages have an obligation to teach?</li> <li>What, other than raw power, decides who is in charge? How are disputes settled?</li> <li>What is an Avatar, and what does Ascension look like?</li> </ol>This is just a sample, but it's the kind of questions I'm having to answer right now. I think if you simply remove the Traditions, you will find your PCs asking these kind of questions of themselves and of the NPC Mages in your game. Even experienced players can find it handy to have a foundation for their character; Traditions provide foundation.<br /> <br /> Human beings generally group themselves around &quot;why&quot; and &quot;who&quot; rather than &quot;how&quot;, so you might find yourself ad hoc rebuilding Traditions anyway. Even if you organize the groups around 100% real-world cultures, you're still building groups with beliefs and goals, and natural player curiosity will result in you needing answers. Both a neo-pagan and a voudon may use the same Practice, but they have very different beliefs about when and how to use that power, and those things make characters, and characters make stories.<br /> <br /> The only way I could see this working out is if the number of Mages in the world is incredibly small. If the world consists of a few characters like Gandalf, Pug, Senex, and Circe, then you scrape by on defining them by how they do magick, and let their individual personalities cover the rest. Essentially, only in a world where there are too few Mages to have a culture can a narrative thrive without Mage culture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T16:52:41-07:00","id":1118000,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Totentanz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1117798#post1117798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my game, I made the decision to replace the Traditions, as I feel they didn't age particularly well. I got more work than I bargained for, really. I didn't fully consider how many decisions I would have to make, and how much writing I would need to do for my players' benefit. I still like where it's going, and it's really pushing me as a ST, but it's work.<br /> <br /> So, while I sympathize with the desire to remove the Traditions, I can't imagine running a game without <b>something </b>to replace them. Practices are a great addition to the game, but they don't answer key questions:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What does the character believe is the appropriate use of magick?</li> <li>What change does the character want to see in the world? Mages by and large are not content with the status quo, and in Mage ambition influences magick.</li> <li>What is the underlying belief structure that fuels their study of magick? Being a Mage is dangerous and difficult; most aren't in it just for personal power. Compare the reasoning of a Verbena to a Euthanatos.</li> <li>What is the method of training apprentices, and do experienced Mages have an obligation to teach?</li> <li>What, other than raw power, decides who is in charge? How are disputes settled?</li> <li>What is an Avatar, and what does Ascension look like?</li> </ol>This is just a sample, but it's the kind of questions I'm having to answer right now. I think if you simply remove the Traditions, you will find your PCs asking these kind of questions of themselves and of the NPC Mages in your game. Even experienced players can find it handy to have a foundation for their character; Traditions provide foundation.<br /> <br /> Human beings generally group themselves around &quot;why&quot; and &quot;who&quot; rather than &quot;how&quot;, so you might find yourself ad hoc rebuilding Traditions anyway. Even if you organize the groups around 100% real-world cultures, you're still building groups with beliefs and goals, and natural player curiosity will result in you needing answers. Both a neo-pagan and a voudon may use the same Practice, but they have very different beliefs about when and how to use that power, and those things make characters, and characters make stories.<br /> <br /> The only way I could see this working out is if the number of Mages in the world is incredibly small. If the world consists of a few characters like Gandalf, Pug, Senex, and Circe, then you scrape by on defining them by how they do magick, and let their individual personalities cover the rest. Essentially, only in a world where there are too few Mages to have a culture can a narrative thrive without Mage culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>Words of wisdom here. I am not a fan of political backstabbing, but rallying together groups of Mages with different outlooks toward a unified goal as underdogs against the Technocracy has generally been a fun concept to me (when I'm not playing a Technocrat, of course). If the Order of Reason/Technocracy metaplot is going to remain (a metaplot I personally enjoy), then I would keep in mind the above comments from Totentanz.<br /> <br /> Dare I suggest that you try CofD's Mage: the Awakening 2nd Edition if you want to strip out Ascension's entire metaplot (including reality being shaped by belief)? If you want a more generic magic system that you can use with your own entirely custom setting, I think nWoD/CofD does it quite a bit better mechanically. You could probably even keep the 'reality shaped by belief/collective belief' bit and still use Awakening (they still have paradox in it; you might just have to slightly tweak those rules to fit the function of Paradox from Ascension).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12988-imperator","name":"Imperator","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-20T08:31:29-07:00","id":1118576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Imperator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1118000#post1118000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Words of wisdom here. I am not a fan of political backstabbing, but rallying together groups of Mages with different outlooks toward a unified goal as underdogs against the Technocracy has generally been a fun concept to me (when I'm not playing a Technocrat, of course). If the Order of Reason/Technocracy metaplot is going to remain (a metaplot I personally enjoy), then I would keep in mind the above comments from Totentanz.<br /> <br /> Dare I suggest that you try CofD's Mage: the Awakening 2nd Edition if you want to strip out Ascension's entire metaplot (including reality being shaped by belief)? If you want a more generic magic system that you can use with your own entirely custom setting, I think nWoD/CofD does it quite a bit better mechanically. You could probably even keep the 'reality shaped by belief/collective belief' bit and still use Awakening (they still have paradox in it; you might just have to slightly tweak those rules to fit the function of Paradox from Ascension).</div> </div> </div> </div>And there's a book out there that can help you do it, even.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-21T08:27:59-07:00","id":1118972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1117561#post1117561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, you don't need the Verbena to have witches, because not all witches are Verbena and not all Verbena are witches. The problem is that mages of a shared Sleeper cultural background are going to congregate over that more than Practices. A Druidic mage practicing Witchcraft, a Druidic mage practicing Faith, and a Druidic mage praticing High Ritual, are going to gather together for their shared Druidism. The Druidic Faith mage isn't going to go hang out with an Evangelical Faith mage because of Faith.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, I guess I hadn't looked at it this way. You make a strong point. I am convinced there does need to be the Traditions. I'm probably still going to do some tweaking, but I see your point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","name":"CrackedMan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1885_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"254"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-21T08:30:08-07:00","id":1118974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Imperator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1117379-ditching-traditions-crafts-for-just-practices?p=1118000#post1118000\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dare I suggest that you try CofD's Mage: the Awakening 2nd Edition if you want to strip out Ascension's entire metaplot (including reality being shaped by belief)? If you want a more generic magic system that you can use with your own entirely custom setting, I think nWoD/CofD does it quite a bit better mechanically. You could probably even keep the 'reality shaped by belief/collective belief' bit and still use Awakening (they still have paradox in it; you might just have to slightly tweak those rules to fit the function of Paradox from Ascension).</div> </div> </div> </div>Wanting to remove the Traditions as organized factions is a far cry from wanting to strip out the entire metaplot and setting. There are things I do like about Awakening, but I definitely want to stick with the basic premise of a belief shaped reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1885-crackedman","name":"CrackedMan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1885_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"254"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}